# The lock screen's question: "is this the same person who is already logged in?"
#
# Shipped HERE rather than in /etc/pam.d, because PamContext takes a
# configDirectory and a lock screen that needs root to install is a lock screen
# that does not exist on a fresh checkout. See services/Lock.qml.
#
# pam_unix reads /etc/shadow through the setuid unix_chkpwd helper, so this
# authenticates correctly from the unprivileged process the shell runs as.
# pam_faillock brackets it, so a machine left alone with someone patient locks
# the account rather than offering unlimited guesses; its counter lives in
# /run/faillock and is the user's own file.
#
# DELIBERATELY NOT `include system-login`. That stack adds pam_shells and
# pam_nologin, which answer "may this account start a session" - a question that
# was settled when the session started. A lock screen asks only whether the
# person in front of it is the same one, and an /etc/nologin dropped by a
# routine update must never be able to make an already-locked screen unopenable.
auth        required                    pam_faillock.so     preauth
auth        [success=1 default=bad]     pam_unix.so         nullok
auth        [default=die]               pam_faillock.so     authfail
auth        required                    pam_faillock.so     authsucc
